TI  - Improved method for comparing abundance data for soil zoological field studies
T2  - EUROPEAN JOURNAL OF SOIL BIOLOGY
AB  - Soil bulk density varies among sites as well as microhabitats, and in different years at the same site. Bulk density is a highly variable parameter that introduces undefined but significant errors to abundance field data of soil microfauna when given in individuals per g soil dry mass (sdm). Such data are not comparable among sites, microhabitats and years and they are unsuitable for comparative ecological field studies. Soil corer sampling technique, defined as soil surface square units (e.g. 1 cm(2)), is recommended to overcome the methodological imprecision of estimating abundance of soil microfauna from field studies in ind.g sdm. This removes consideration of variable soil bulk density and enables us to establish abundance values directly in ind.cm(-2) without any recalculations. Resultant data are comparable both spatially and temporally. Abundance data as individuals per m(2) is a commonly used unit for soil meso-, macro- and megafauna. (C) 2000 Editions scientifiques et medicales Elsevier SAS.
