TI  - Initial data on the biology of Sphodroxia maroccana Ley (Coleoptera: Melolonthidae) and an estimation of the damage due to this new pest on cork-oak seedlings in the Mamora Forest (Morocco)
T2  - Entomological Research in Mediterranean Forest Ecosystems
CP  - International Symposium on Entomological Research in Mediterranean Forest Ecosystems
AB  - The Mamora Forest (130,000 ha) extends over a distance of 70 km between Rabat and Kenitra in Morocco. Very deteriorated in some locations, the natural vegetation consists mainly of cork-oak (Quercus suber). At present, the natural regeneration of cork-oak is very difficult due to exacerbated anthropogenic effects (e.g., harvesting of acorns and wood for human consumption, grazing), and the forest is also changing due to the introduction of exotic tree species (mostly pine and eucalyptus). The regeneration of artificial cork-oak by seedling acorns and by transplanting one-year old trees previously grown in nurseries is subject to a very high mortality rate in certain areas, ranging between 41 and 68% during the first year. The different causes of seedling death are white grubs, summer drought and decline and poor weeding practices. The mortality due to white grubs ranges between 24 and 43%, depending on the plots. The larvae damage seedlings below the collar level. Both field and laboratory experiments showed that the damage was due to the larvae of two species, the Dynastid beetle Phyllognathus excavatus (Forster) to a lesser extent and the Melolonthid beetle Sphodroxia maroccana Ley, that was responsible for most of the damage. The female of S. maroccana, a species endemic to Morocco, was previously completely unknown and will be the subject of a paper to be published in the near future (paper in preparation). The breeding period occurs in summer (July). The females, twice as big as the males, are unable to fly. During a period of three to four hours, from the end of the afternoon until dusk, the females attract the males with a sex pheromone and mate at the entrance of tunnels dug in the sandy soil. Females oviposit 60-85 eggs during their lifetime. The time necessary for larval development is estimated at three to five years. Anthropogenic degradation of the forest (mostly grazing) is undoubtedly one of the causes of the recent infestation of S. maroccana in the Mamora Forest.
