TI  - Newly Introduced Aphid is Associated with Lower Growth and Reproduction of Invasive Garlic Mustard
T2  - NATURAL AREAS JOURNAL
AB  - The success of invasive species can be facilitated by a lack of natural enemies in their introduced ranges. Garlic mustard (Alliaria petiolata) is a highly invasive biennial herb found in forests across North America, where it reduces species diversity by displacing native understory plants. Originally native to Europe, garlic mustard is now experiencing herbivore pressure from the garlic mustard aphid (Lipaphis alliariae), an insect native to Europe that was first observed in North America in 2021. To determine if the newly introduced garlic mustard aphid has the potential to be detrimental to garlic mustard in North America, we assessed naturally occurring garlic mustard plants, quantifying the differences in growth and reproduction among garlic mustard plants found with and without aphids. While there were no differences in stem number, leaf number, or seed viability between plants found with and without garlic mustard aphids, plants found with aphids were shorter and had lower aboveground biomass. In addition, plants with aphids produced fewer and smaller seeds. Given that garlic mustard plants found with aphids were less robust, we recommend further manipulative experiments to determine the potential impacts of the garlic mustard aphid on this highly invasive plant.
