TI  - Defoliation facilitates Leymus chinensis clones spatial expansion into saline-alkali soils under different population densities
T2  - FLORA
AB  - Defoliation/grazing and competition could have impacts on growth and spatial expansion of clonal plants. However, how these factors influencing clonal plants spatial expansion in heterogeneous environments remain ambiguous. A pot experiment was conducted to examine the effects of defoliation, intraspecific competition and their interactions on the spatial expansion of Leymus chinensis (Trin.) Tzvel., a dominant clonal plant species in the Songnen grassland, northeast China, from favourable soil patches to saline-alkali soil patches. Results showed that defoliation significantly increased L. chinensis spatial expansion from favourable soil patches into saline -alkali ones. The average rhizome length increased, as did the number of ramets growing in saline-alkali soil patches. Intraspecific competition had no documented effects on these parameters. Defoliation increased the total biomass of the ramets in moderate and severe saline-alkali patches by 33% and 22%, respectively. Across the saline-alkali stress regimes, defoliation increased the belowground biomass by 26%, while increased aboveground biomass by 18%. Results demonstrated that defoliation improved L. chinensis expansion in saline -alkali soil patches mainly due to increased belowground biomass, which contributes to rhizome elongation and ramet proliferation. Therefore, it is suggested that defoliation/mowing management practices in Songnen degraded grasslands might promote the expansion and colonization of L. chinensis clones toward degraded saline -alkali patches, thereby accelerating vegetative reconstruction and grassland restoration.
