TI  - Contrasting effects of short- and long-term grazing exclusion on plant diversity in humid grasslands
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Grazing exclusion (GE) is widely considered one of the most cost-effective methods to restore vegetation in degraded grasslands; however, its effects on plant diversity in humid grasslands remain unclear. Here, we synthesized previous studies to test whether the effects of exclusion on plant diversity were different between arid and humid grasslands. Furthermore, the effects of enclosure duration on plant diversity were explored in humid grasslands using three enclosure ages (6 months, 1 year and 6 years), each paired with adjacent grazed areas. Meta-regression revealed significant negative effects of aridity index on the effect sizes of GE on plant diversity indices, and indicated that the impact of GE shifted from positive in drier grasslands to negative in wetter grasslands. Field research further confirmed that long-term GE (1 year and 6 years) decreased plant richness and evenness in humid grasslands. This reduction was mainly due to the competitive exclusion of subordinate species by a few proliferating dominant grasses, resulting in an increase in their dominance. On the contrary, short-term GE (6 months) significantly increased plant richness, mainly attributed to the rapid recovery of grass richness after GE. Our findings highlight that the year-scale study may mask the effect of GE on plant diversity in humid grasslands, and also provide a strategic framework for optimising GE duration to restore plant diversity. More research exploring GE effects at finer timescales is needed to optimise management strategies for grassland recovery in humid regions.
