TI  - Grazing intensity and duration modulate the biotic refuge effect on community reproductive output
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Unpalatable plants can provide biotic refuges for their neighbors under grazing, thus playing crucial protective roles in community reproductive output, which is essential for maintaining community diversity and structure. However, the synergistic responses of unpalatable species population and its biotic refuge effect on community reproductive output to grazing intensity and duration remain poorly understood. Here, we conducted a four grazing intensities experiment for three consecutive years to examine the synergistic responses of Morina kokonorica population and its biotic refuge effect on community reproductive output to grazing intensity and duration. The results showed that M. kokonorica provided biotic refuges for the community reproductive output, primarily by alleviating the negative impacts of grazing on the reproductive branches of dominant Poaceae species. Notably, these refuge effects were more pronounced under heavy grazing. Communities with M. kokonorica exhibited 1.4 times, 5.6 times, and 4.9 times higher numbers of harvested species, reproductive branches, and seeds, respectively, compared to those without M. kokonorica under heavy grazing in 2020. Moreover, we provide the first evidence that the biotic refuge effect depends on grazing duration. As grazing duration extended, the defense area and population of M. kokonorica diminished, ultimately leading to collapses of biotic refuge effects. Our findings emphasize the critical role of grazing duration alongside intensity in shaping biotic refuge effect, which will promote a reconsideration of the ecological role of unpalatable species in grazing management, especially in grassland restoration. Unpalatable plants emerge as a self-sustaining mechanism in grassland ecosystems, offering vital biotic refuges for the community reproductive output. Consequently, management strategies that focus on the eradication of unpalatable plants may not only fail to restore degraded grasslands, but may exacerbate degradation due to the removal of biotic refuges.
