TI  - Species diversity and ecological strategies shifted in degraded alpine grasslands: insights from the Yellow River source region
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Alpine grasslands dominated the ecosystem of the Yellow River source region, yet their degradation posed significant ecological challenges. This study aimed to elucidate the adaptive mechanisms and ecological processes of alpine grassland plant communities in response to degradation and identify the key characteristics and driving factors of alpine grassland degradation by comparing non-degraded and degraded sites. Plant and soil samples from 144 locations were collected and redundancy analysis (RDA) and multiple linear regression were utilized in this study. The results revealed that degraded grasslands exhibited significantly lower species diversity, functional diversity and soil nutrient contents, alongside elevated soil pH. Furthermore, the ecological strategies of plant communities shifted from ruderal (R) strategists to stress-tolerant (S) strategists following degradation. Soil pH and total nitrogen were identified as the primary factors influencing grassland degradation. These results highlight the importance of controlled grazing, nitrogen and phosphorus fertilization, and comprehensive soil alkalinity management for the effective restoration and sustainable management of degraded alpine grasslands in the Yellow River source region.
