AU  - ROONEY, TP
TI  - RESTORING LANDSCAPE DIVERSITY AND OLD-GROWTH TO PENNSYLVANIA NORTHERN HARDWOOD FORESTS
T2  - NATURAL AREAS JOURNAL
AB  - The climax vegetation of the northern hardwood forest community in Pennsylvania was shaped by soils, topography, and disturbance regimes. Disturbances on xeric sites often resulted in even-aged stands, whereas disturbances on mesic sites created a complex, uneven-aged forest. The extensive clearing of these forests around 1900 resulted in the elimination of the old-growth landscaper matrix. Intensified, human-mediated disturbance regimes have been superimposed upon the forest; this has resulted in a loss of heterogeneity across the landscape. Throughout much of northern Pennsylvania, the second-growth forest lacks an understory, and for the past 50 years, woody recruitment has been hindered by the direct and indirect effects of deer browsing. Coarse woody debris is largely absent. To restore landscape-level diversity to these forests, deer densities must be reduced. In addition, horizontal and vertical diversity, as well as coarse woody debris, must be restored to these forests. This might best be accomplished by establishing several large old-growth recovery areas linked through a biosphere reserve network. Since public landholdings dominate much of northern Pennsylvania's land base, such a strategy is feasible and may be effective in restoring landscape diversity.
