TI  - Community characteristics in a mountain forest invaded by Gleditsia triacanthos
T2  - PLANT INVASIONS: SPECIES ECOLOGY AND ECOSYSTEM MANAGEMENT
CP  - 5th International Conference on Ecology of Invasive Alien Plants
AB  - Gleditsia triacanthos L. (Fabaceae) is originaly from the central-east USA. and has invaded the mountain forest of San Lorenzo (Salta, Argentina) in association with anthropogenic disturbances. This work is part of a major research and is focused on community features related to the invasion process. We studied the relative abundance, species richness, similarity and diameter (as DBH) of trees in three successional stages: colonized, mixed and mature. The three situations were different in the environmental and vegetational characteristics. Invasion occurs in gaps created for cattle grazing. G. triacanthos is the most abundant species in colonized and mixed stages but in the latter we also found abundant recruitment of native species. We did not find individuals of the introduced species in the mature stage. Tree species abundance model was geometric in colonized stages and logarithmic in the mixed and mature stages. Diameter was different between successional stages. We discuss these results and relate them to the successional stages, the invasion process, forest regeneration and the intrinsic features of the invasive species.
