TI  - Microbially-driven mine restoration via functional redundancy: Mechanisms sustaining rhizospheric microbiome homeostasis in lianas
T2  - RHIZOSPHERE
AB  - Rhizosphere microbiota serves as microscopic engines driving ecological restoration, where their functional redundancy and homeostatic thresholds critically determine vegetation stress resilience and restoration efficiency. To decipher the microbial-driven mechanisms of lianas in mine revegetation, this study targeted an abandoned iron mine in Longmen, Guangdong, investigating naturally colonized Smilax china L., Paederia foetida L., and Pericampylus glaucus (Lam.) Merr. through integrated soil physicochemical analysis (pH, organic matter, total N/P/K, etc.) and high-throughput sequencing (16S rRNA/ITS2). Key findings reveal that Smilax china L. rhizosphere exhibited significantly higher organic matter (5.53 g/kg), total nitrogen (0.47 g/kg), total phosphorus (0.20 g/kg), and alkali-hydrolyzable nitrogen (44.53 mg/kg) compared to other species. Chloroflexota emerged as the dominant bacterial phylum in Smilax china L. and Pericampylus glaucus (Lam.) Merr. (relative abundance >35 %), with its high abundance correlating with elevated bacterial Shannon diversity (index 6.39). Ascomycota predominated among fungi, while Smilax demonstrated optimal fungal evenness (Pielou index 0.82). LEfSe analysis identified enrichment of heavy metal-resistant Pseudolabrys (LDA >2.0) and plant-growth-promoting Exobasidiomycetes (LDA >3.0) in Smilax china L. rhizosphere. Strong positive correlations between total N/P and Planctomycetota (r > 0.83, p < 0.01) confirmed nutrient-driven microbial assembly. These results demonstrate that lianas enhance stress resistance through rhizosphere microbial functional redundancy (high OTU richness) and homeostatic thresholds (signature species), with their nutrient enrichment effects and keystone microbial taxa providing critical engineering targets for mine vegetation restoration.
