TI  - Cattle grazing controls non-native grass invasion and restores ground layer diversity and structure in tropical open ecosystems
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Biological invasion is a major challenge for conserving and restoring Cerrado open ecosystems, with cattle grazing suggested as a nature-based solution to control invasive species. However, empirical studies are needed to provide evidence-based recommendations for this management practice. We investigated the effects of cattle grazing (rotation grazing system) on an invaded grassland using a Before-After-Control-Impact design, a standard method to measure intervention impact. We assessed vegetation structure, composition, and diversity before and after two years in grazed compared to ungrazed plots (100 1-m(2) plots per treatment). Floristic composition changed after grazing. Total richness and ground layer richness were 20 % and 30 % higher, respectively, in grazed than in ungrazed plots. Invasive and native grass cover decreased, while forbs, subshrubs, and shrubs increased. Of the 111 species analyzed, 16 % were disadvantaged, 58 % were unaffected, and 26 % benefited from cattle grazing. Species with root crowns and taproots were resilient to grazing, as their underground organs can resprout following grazing. Grazing effectively reduced invasive grass biomass and increased bare soil to reference levels of pristine grasslands. When properly managed, grazing offers more benefits than disadvantages to grassland vegetation. Additionally, it is the most profitable and sustainable land use permitted in "legal reserves" required by federal law in the Cerrado, where native vegetation must be preserved or restored. Grazing should be considered a nature-based solution for open ecosystems conservation and restoration, provided that evidence-based guidelines for "how to graze" ensure ecological and economic sustainability of the system.
