TI  - Slight degradation significantly alters plant functional groups and biomass accumulation in alpine meadow on the eastern Tibetan Plateau
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Grassland degradation impacts plant community structure and productivity, yet its underlying mechanisms are not fully understood. This study examined plant community properties across 64 alpine meadow sites on the eastern Tibetan Plateau, spanning four degradation stages (C I - C IV). Results showed that plant diversity increased with degradation, particularly at C II and C III stages. However, both aboveground (AGB) and belowground (BGB) biomass decreased significantly (p < 0.05), with total biomass declining from 3995 to 2212 g m(-)(2). Degradation also increased community complexity, reduced network modularity, and shifted plant functional groups (PFGs). Forbs replaced the co-dominance of grasses, sedges and forbs, becoming the sole dominant PFG in degraded sites. Key environmental factors influencing community properties varied with degradation. Partial least squares path modeling revealed that degradation reduced community biomass accumulation primarily by altering the PFGs, and depleting soil water and nutrient content. These findings suggest that while degradation increases plant diversity, it significantly reduces biomass, particularly the AGB of grasses and sedges, even at early stages (coverage >85 %). Therefore, targeted restoration strategies are essential to protect key PFGs, such as grasses and sedges, to sustain grassland ecosystem services.
