TI  - Grassland restoration increases soil C release rates but reduces its temperature sensitivity at the topmost and subsurface layers
T2  - AGRICULTURE ECOSYSTEMS & ENVIRONMENT
AB  - Grassland restoration can promote soil carbon (C) sequestration function and modulate C dynamics, yet soil depth-dependent heterogeneity in the process controlling C mineralization and its temperature sensitivity (Q10) remained overlooked, creating uncertainty in forecasting C-climate feedback. To reveal the drivers of these processes, we sampled soils from topmost (0-10 cm) and subsurface (10-30 cm) layers across a regional area (including fencing and grazing) in temperate grasslands of China. We conducted short-term laboratory incubations combined with soil and microbial analyses to identify the key drivers of soil C release and Q10. We found that fencing increased soil C release rates from the topmost and subsurface soils by 4.11 % and 3.40 %, respectively, while reducing their Q10 coefficients (1.20 and 1.24, respectively) by 7.47 % and 3.08 % compared to grazed grassland. Concomitantly, the low Q10 at the topmost layer induced by fencing was modulated by strong C physical protection coupled with low bacterial diversity, whereas an increase in microbial biomass C (MBC) and limiting N dominated topmost soil C release under grassland restoration. The lower Q10 at the subsurface layer under fencing was attributed to promoting the "N mining" effect, while an increase in fungal diversity dominated subsurface C release under grassland restoration. These findings emphasized that grassland restoration induced distinct depth-dependent mechanisms (N pathways and microbial diversity) governing soil C dynamics, which were vital for moving beyond bulk soil estimates and building predictive models that accurately predicted grassland C cycling in response to global warming.
