TI  - Fish communities in artificial pools in the floodplain along the lower reaches of the River Dyje
T2  - FOLIA ZOOLOGICA
AB  - In 1995 and 1996 the fish communities inhabiting nine artificial pools were investigated in the floodplain along :he lower reaches of the River Dyje. In the habitats under study, evidence has been obtained of the occurrence of 23 species and two interspecific hybrids (Table 1). Esox lucius, Rutilus rutilus, Scardinius erythrophthalmus, Tinca tinca, Blicca bjoerkna, Abramis brama, Rhodeus sericeus, Carassius auratus, Misgurnus fossilis and Perca fluviatilis were evaluated as the characteristic components of fish communities inhabiting aquatic habitats in the floodplain along the lower reaches of the River Dyje. The previous keystone species, Tinca tinca and especially Carassius carassius, have been recorded only occasionally and their restoration is considered. The present occurrence of fish species and the species composition of the fish communities under study are the result of natural development during the past decade. Natural floods following excessively increased water discharges in the River Dyje are of decisive importance in the formation of fish communities in the pools under study. The data obtained indicate that artificial permanent pools can fully compensate for the previous natural aquatic habitats such as pools, river arms and little lakes that had developed through the fluvial activity of the river and that were later permanently eliminated in its lower reaches.
