TI  - Ecological resilience enhanced by the Ten-Year Fishing Ban: Evidence from network-based analysis of fish community recovery in the Yangtze River Estuary
T2  - REGIONAL STUDIES IN MARINE SCIENCE
AB  - The Yangtze River Estuary, a vital ecological transition zone linking freshwater and marine environments, has undergone severe degradation due to decades of overfishing, habitat fragmentation, and environmental stressors. In response, China initiated a basin-wide Ten-Year Fishing Ban in 2021 to support fishery resource recovery. This study analyzed fish community responses from 2018 to 2023 using Abundance-Biomass Comparison (ABC) curves and ecological network analysis. A total of 58 fish species were recorded, encompassing 13 orders and 23 families. Results revealed substantial temporal variation in community stability. Prior to the ban (2018-2020), assemblages were dominated by small, r-selected species, with simplified structure and high disturbance levels. Post-ban, biomass gradually exceeded abundance, and the W statistic became positive, indicating a transition toward more stable, K-selected communities. However, a decrease in W in 2023 suggested renewed fluctuations during recovery. Ecological network analysis demonstrated increased food web complexity, network density, and clustering coefficients after the ban, alongside the emergence of new keystone species. Yet, a decline in connectivity and rising fragmentation in 2023 signaled ongoing structural reorganization. Overall, the fishing ban has partially promoted structural and functional recovery in the estuarine fish community, but the system remains in a dynamic adjustment phase. To ensure long-term restoration, it is crucial to uphold rigorous policy enforcement, enhance ecological monitoring, and implement integrated habitat management across the estuarine ecosystem.
