TI  - RESEARCH ON OBTAINING A SUBSTITUT TOPSOIL FOR ECOLOGICAL RECONSTRUCTION OF MINING WASTE DUMPS
T2  - ECOLOGY, ECONOMICS, EDUCATION AND LEGISLATION CONFERENCE PROCEEDINGS, SGEM 2016, VOL I
CP  - 16th International Multidisciplinary Scientific Geoconference (SGEM 2016)
AB  - Mining waste dumps are engineering constructions and they represent accumulations of sterile rocks resulting from opening and preparation works, for underground mines, or the overburden of productive complexes, for open pits and quarries. They usually occupy large areas of land, that are either removed from the productive circuit either they have negative effects on long-term over natural ecosystems. For these reasons, ecological reconstruction works must be carried out as soon as they are released of technological tasks.
   The major problem related to the ecological restoration of mining waste dumps is represented by the absence of fertility elements in the stored material composition that can ensure a rapid resettlement of vegetation (spontaneously) or allow the re-cultivation of these areas of land.
   In this context, this paper presents a series of investigations that were aimed at obtaining a topsoil replacement by using mining waste rocks and compost obtained from the organic fraction of household waste, capable of ensuring necessary conditions for ecological restoration.
