TI  - EFFECTS OF ALTITUDE ON THE ABUNDANCE AND SPECIES RICHNESS OF INSECT HERBIVORES ON BRACKEN
T2  - JOURNAL OF ANIMAL ECOLOGY
AB  - (1) The numbers of species of insects feeding on bracken (Pteridium aquilinum (L.) Kuhn.) were recorded at 213 sites at different altitudes throughout mainland Britain, UK. Herbivore species richness and abundance were also recorded in five detailed altitudinal transects of the North Yorkshire Moors. (2) Only one species, the delphacid bug Ditropis pteridis (Spin.), had a distribution that was clearly and consistently linked with altitude. As altitude increased, it occurred at fewer sites and became less abundant at occupied sites. (3) Ditropis was introduced in two consecutive years onto bracken above its normal altitudinal range. It reproduced successfully over the summer, but failed to overwinter, possibly because it could not synchronize its life-history with delayed emergence of bracken fronds in the spring at high altitudes. (4) Unlike the results of other similar studies, the total number of species of bracken herbivores did not decline with altitude. As a resource for  its insect herbivores, bracken in relatively similar (though not identical) at all altitudes. We were also able to control for a number of other habitat variables that may influence insect species richness over altitudinal gradients (e.g. declining host-plant species richness and reduced habitat area at high altitudes). Therefore, these other variables, rather than increasing environmental harshness, may be primarily responsible for the decline in insect species richness that is often observed with increasing altitude.
