TI  - AN EXPERIMENTAL INVESTIGATION OF INTERRELATIONSHIPS BETWEEN THE WOOD-ANT (FORMICA-RUFA) AND SOME TREE-CANOPY HERBIVORES
T2  - JOURNAL OF ANIMAL ECOLOGY
AB  - Previous work on interactions between F. rufa and some canopy herbivores is briefly reviewed. In limestone woodland in north England, populations of defoliators (illustrated by the winter moth, Operophtera brumata L.), a non-tended aphid species (Drepanosiphum platanoides Schr.) and a tended aphid species (Periphyllus testudinaceus Fernie) are all affected by the presence of the ant. Predation of D. platanoidis by F. rufa results in significant depression of the population compared with experimental conditions where ants were excluded from shoots. Trees without ants have significantly higher populations of Lepidoptera larvae than do foraged trees. On trees with ants an average of 1% of the leaf area had been removed by defoliators at the end of the season; without ants the total was nearly 8%. E. rufa tended 8 aphid species [Tortrix viridana, Lachnus roboris, Cinara sp., Aphis urtica, A. sambuchi, Thelaxes sp., Betulaphis quadrituberculata] and 1 psyllid [Psylla crataegi] in the woodland. The association of E. rufa with 1 of the aphids (P. testudinaceus) was studied. P. testudinaceus populations were significantly increased in the presence of ants. When ants were excluded from colonies on shoots a significant fall in numbers occurred. There was probably also a reduction in the proportion of alatae. The results are discussed in relation to previous work on aphid predation by ants and to the practice of introducing ants into woodland to protect trees from the effects of defoliators. This practice may be ill-founded.
