TI  - DEMOGRAPHIC RESPONSE OF EXPERIMENTAL SNOWSHOE HARE POPULATIONS TO OVERWINTER FOOD SHORTAGE
T2  - JOURNAL OF WILDLIFE MANAGEMENT
AB  - Demographic responses of enclosed snowshoe hare (L. americanus) populations to overwinter food shortage were studied during 1974-1977 near Rochester, Alberta [Canada]. Hares were introduced at 2 levels of density into 8 enclosures (2.9-5.8 ha) of natural habitat during fall. Commercial rabbit food was supplied ad lib to half the enclosures, representing both density levels. Available native browse in the other half was insufficient to support initial fall populations over winter. The 2 density levels averaged 13.2 and 4.2 hares/ha at the start of each experiment. There were 4 treatments (high- and low-density .times. abundant- and scarce-food), each replicated 3-7 times over the 3-yr period. Numbers, weight changes and survival were monitored during Dec.-May; remaining individuals were sacrificed in summer for reproductive data. Mean winter weights were lower and overwinter weight losses greater among hares in scarce-food treatments. Adult survival was slightly reduced by winter food shortage, but juvenile survival was markedly lower. All major components of reproduction (onset and termination of breeding, pregnancy rates, ovulation and implantation rates) were affected by food scarcity during the preceding winter, resulting in shorter breeding seasons and a reduction in mean natality from 18.4-8.5 young/female surviving the breeding season. Weight gains among juvenils born to parents from scarce-food treatments averaged 9.89/day compared to 12.1 g/day among those whose parents had abundant food over winter. Hares that were in scarce-food pens during winter had heavier (P < 0.01) adrenals during May-Aug. A density effect on the demographic attributes of the experimental population was not evident. This feeding experiment supports the view that cyclic declines in snowshoe hare populations are initiated by winter food shortage.
